  "text": "A bench‑scale biological treatment unit was run to verify earlier field work that showed a modest‑size, continuously recirculating system could successfully handle raw domestic waste from a small group of people for half a year, while producing an outflow suitable for toilet flushing. The experiment also recorded how the system behaved when overloaded and how a rise in alkalinity increased unpleasant smells, highlighting the need to keep the acidity between about six and seven. The incoming organic load, measured at roughly 44,000 milligrams per litre (with a related oxygen demand of about 21,000 milligrams per litre), was lowered by close to ninety percent. The resulting water reuse potential for flushing was estimated to exceed ninety percent."
